<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>GoSlicing - Tag - loommii</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/tags/goslicing/</link><description>我的个人技术博客</description><generator>Hugo 0.165.0 &amp; FixIt v0.4.5</generator><language>en</language><lastBuildDate>Fri, 16 Jan 2026 01:28:43 +0800</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://loommii.github.io/en/tags/goslicing/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Go Daily Question 218</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_218/</link><pubDate>Wed, 30 Apr 2025 00:03:19 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_218/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;What are the length and capacity of slices a, b, and c respectively?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;func main() {&#10; s := [3]int{1, 2, 3}&#10; a := s[:0]&#10; b := s[:2]&#10; c := s[1:2:cap(s)]&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;a: len 0, cap 3&#10;b: len 2, cap 3&#10;c: len 1, cap 2&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;c starts at index 1.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Reference Answer and Analysis: The length and capacity of a, b, and c are 0 3, 2 3, and 1 2, respectively.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 156</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_156/</link><pubDate>Thu, 09 Jan 2025 20:27:59 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_156/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;What are the length and capacity of slices a, b, and c respectively?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;package main&#10;&#10;import (&#10; &amp;#34;fmt&amp;#34;&#10;)&#10;&#10;func main() {&#10; s := [3]int{1, 2, 3}&#10; a := s[:0]&#10; b := s[:2]&#10; c := s[1:2:cap(s)]&#10;&#10; fmt.Println(len(a), cap(a))&#10; fmt.Println(len(b), cap(b))&#10; fmt.Println(len(c), cap(c))&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;a: Length and capacity 0 3&#10;b: Length and capacity 2 3&#10;c: Length and capacity 1 2&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://go.dev/play/p/QHiuY9qh4pw" target="_blank" rel="external nofollow noopener noreferrer"&gt;Run online&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h3 class="heading-element" id="key-takeaway-array-or-slice-slicing-operations"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Key Takeaway: Array or Slice Slicing Operations&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#key-takeaway-array-or-slice-slicing-operations" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p&gt;Slicing operations can have 2 or 3 parameters, such as &lt;code&gt;[i:j]&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;[i:j:k]&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 115</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_115/</link><pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 23:42:56 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_115/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;Which of the following calls to the &lt;code&gt;add()&lt;/code&gt; function are correct?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;func add(args ...int) int {&#10; sum := 0&#10; for _, arg := range args {&#10; sum &amp;#43;= arg&#10; }&#10; return sum&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;A. add(1, 2)&#10;B. add(1, 3, 7)&#10;C. add([]int{1, 2})&#10;D. add([]int{1, 3, 7}&amp;hellip;)&#10;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;Reference Answer and Analysis: ABD.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Key takeaway: Variadic functions.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/details&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 084</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_084/</link><pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 20:45:10 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_084/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;What is the output of the following code? Why?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;func main() {&#10; s1 := []int{1, 2, 3}&#10; s2 := s1[1:]&#10; s2[1] = 4&#10; fmt.Println(s1)&#10; s2 = append(s2, 5, 6, 7)&#10; fmt.Println(s1)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;s1 [1,2,4]&#10;s2 [2,4,5,6,7]&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/details&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>